# S2-006 — the retrieval eval scores a retriever that is not on the request path
#
# This finding began as a suspected S1 ("cross-lingual retrieval returns nothing
# for ES/FR eligibility questions"). Measuring it end to end REFUTED that. What
# survives is a different and narrower defect: the gate measures the wrong thing.
# Both halves are recorded here, because the refutation is the more important half.

## 1. What the eval reports

$ python -m evals.run --retrieval        (60 cases with expected ids, k=3)
  overall hit_rate 0.95   mrr 0.9056
  en  n=20  hit 1.00   mrr 0.9417
  es  n=20  hit 0.90   mrr 0.8750
  fr  n=20  hit 0.95   mrr 0.9000
  grounded n=42 hit 1.00
  exact    n=18 hit 0.8333
  misses: es-x2 (ASP-013), es-x4 (ASP-031, got []), fr-x4 (ASP-031, got [])

## 2. Why those misses look alarming

ASP-031 is the eligibility cut-off row:
  "Eligibility covers young people who are currently aged 5 to 18, as well as
   those who were 18 or under on 13 December 2023..."

The same question, scored through `build_retriever`, against the same corpus:

  RETRIEVER_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.2  ->  effective similarity floor = 0.434315

  [en] What does the 13 December 2023 date mean for eligibility?
        ASP-031   similarity=0.7026  [PASS]  <-- TARGET
        => 1 of top-3 survive the floor
  [es] ¿Qué significa la fecha del 13 de diciembre de 2023?
        ASP-031   similarity=0.3837  [cut]   <-- TARGET
        => 0 of top-3 survive  ** NOTHING RETRIEVED **
  [fr] Que signifie la date du 13 décembre 2023 ?
        ASP-031   similarity=0.4011  [cut]   <-- TARGET
        => 0 of top-3 survive  ** NOTHING RETRIEVED **

ASP-031 ranks FIRST in all three languages — cross-lingual ranking is fine. It is
the absolute-similarity floor that cuts it: cross-lingual pairs score ~0.38-0.40
where same-language pairs score ~0.70, and 0.434315 sits between those bands.

## 3. Why it is NOT a product bug — measured, cold cache

The product does not use `build_retriever`. `app/agents/qa/nodes.py`:
  rewrite_query -> hybrid_retrieve -> rerank -> generate -> ground_check
with RRF fusion of a vector and a lexical retriever, and a ground_check node that
rejects an answer citing nothing or containing an unretrieved figure.

Asked through the real transport, POST /v2/chat/stream, with a UNIQUE cache
namespace so nothing could be a hit:

  [es] agent=qa_agent_public cached=None elapsed=10154ms
     "La fecha del **13 de diciembre de 2023** define parte del grupo elegible:
      también pueden participar los jóvenes que tenían **18 años o menos** en esa fecha."

  [fr] agent=qa_agent_public cached=None elapsed=4742ms
     "La date du **13 décembre 2023** sert de date de référence pour l'éligibilité.
      Les jeunes qui avaient **18 ans ou moins** à cette date peuvent bénéficier d'ASPIRE."

Both factually correct against ASP-031. Cross-lingual retrieval WORKS in production.

## 4. The finding that survives

$ grep -n "evals.run" .github/workflows/evals.yml
46:  run: uv run python -m evals.run --verify-ids
62:  run: uv run python -m evals.run --retrieval --fail-under 0.95 --json evals-retrieval.json

CI gates the nightly eval on a retriever that no request touches. Three consequences:

  a. Its 3 reported misses are FALSE ALARMS. The product answers all three correctly.
  b. Its 0.95 is not a measure of product retrieval quality, so the number cannot
     be used to argue the product is or is not regressing.
  c. A real regression in hybrid_retrieve / rerank / ground_check is INVISIBLE here.

And it is brittle: hit_rate is 0.95 against --fail-under 0.95, exactly on the line.
One more cross-lingual case scored this way turns the nightly red for a reason that
does not correspond to any user-visible defect.

## 5. Coverage note on the golden set

$ python -m evals.run --verify-ids
75 cases, 20 distinct expected ids
all expected ids exist in the CSV

60 cases with expectations cover 20 distinct rows of a 706-row corpus (2.8%).
